# [Add the dependency]($heading.id('add-the-dependency'))

sqlz needs Zig 0.17.0 or newer. Add it to your `build.zig.zon`:

```console
$ zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/westack-it/sqlz
```

>[]($block.attrs('note'))
>sqlz has no tagged release yet. `zig fetch` pins the current commit; update
>it deliberately, since the API is still moving.

# [Wire up the build]($heading.id('wire-up-the-build'))

Enable the backends you need and register one sqlz project. The check step
runs the checker on the host and generates the `queries` module:

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const sqlz_build = @import("sqlz");

pub fn build(b: *std.Build) void {
    const target = b.standardTargetOptions(.{});
    const sqlz_dep = b.dependency("sqlz", .{ .sqlite = true });

    const tool = sqlz_build.addTool(b, .{ .dependency = sqlz_dep });
    const db = tool.addProject(.{
        .name = "app",
        .config = b.path("sqlz.ziggy"),
        .runtime = .{ .sqlite = true },
    });

    const exe = b.addExecutable(.{
        .name = "app",
        .root_module = b.createModule(.{
            .root_source_file = b.path("src/main.zig"),
            .target = target,
        }),
    });
    exe.root_module.addImport("sqlz", db.runtime_module);
    exe.root_module.addImport("queries", db.queries_module);
    exe.step.dependOn(db.check_step);
    b.installArtifact(exe);
}
```

# [Describe the project]($heading.id('describe-the-project'))

`sqlz.ziggy` sits next to `build.zig` and tells the checker where migrations
and queries live:

```ziggy
.format_version = 1,
.project_id = "8f1f2f7c-6f2a-4f5a-9a4a-1d6f3b5c7e21",
.migrations = "migrations",
.sql_roots = .{ .app = "queries" },
.zig_roots = ["src"],
.backends = .{
  .sqlite = .{ .profile = "3.53" },
},
```

Add a first migration in `migrations/aaaaaaaaaaaa_init/`:

```ziggy
// revision.ziggy
.format_version = 1,
.revision = "aaaaaaaaaaaa",
.parents = [],
.description = "create users",
.created_utc = "2026-11-27T12:00:00Z",
.backends = [.sqlite],
.reversible = true,
.transaction = .{ .sqlite = .always },
```

```sql
-- common.up.sql
CREATE TABLE users(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL);
```

```sql
-- common.down.sql
DROP TABLE users;
```

# [Write a query]($heading.id('write-a-query'))

Each `.sql` file under `queries/` is one named query. The header comments
name it, choose its backends and say how many rows it returns:

```sql
-- queries/users/find_user.sql
-- sqlz.name: find_user
-- sqlz.backends: sqlite
-- sqlz.cardinality: optional

SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE id = :id;
```

# [Run it]($heading.id('run-it'))

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const sqlz = @import("sqlz");
const find_user = @import("queries").app.users.find_user;

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !void {
    var conn = try sqlz.sqlite.open(init.gpa, init.io, "app.db", .{});
    defer conn.deinit();

    var found = (try sqlz.unwrap(find_user.fetchOptional(&conn, .{ .id = 1 }))) orelse
        return error.UserMissing;
    defer found.deinit();
    std.log.info("user: {s}", .{found.row().name});
}
```

Now break the query, for example by selecting a column that doesn't exist,
and run `zig build`. The checker rejects it before anything is compiled.

Next, read how [checked queries]($link.page('docs/guides/queries')) work, or
browse the runnable programs in
[`examples/`](https://github.com/westack-it/sqlz/tree/main/examples).
